PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Colleagues split 3 ways (A/B/C). Pro-C (1,10,12): symmetry partitions profiles by k=# choosing action 1; NE conditions on best-reply thresholds produce two contiguous blocks of admissible k that together cover {0..n}, so ≥2 distinct k (hence ≥2 pure profiles) always exist; Chicken realizes exactly 2. Pro-B (3,4,6,7,15-18): at least one unanimous profile (k=0 or k=n) must be NE because contradictory payoff inequalities otherwise; PD realizes exactly 1. Pro-A (2,5,8,13,14): symmetric matching-pennies style payoffs can make every profile unstable.  
My prior leaned B (PD example). After integrating, the block-cover argument is decisive: the two threshold conditions on BR(m) are complementary across the entire interval and cannot leave it empty; hence zero impossible and one impossible. Bound tight at exactly 2. Unresolved: only whether indifference points can collapse the count to 1, but generic strict payoffs rule that out.  
}

